import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

@RestControllerAdvice(basePackageClasses = BillingExceptionHandler.class)
public class BillingExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotFound(InvoiceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_FOUND", ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotCancellableException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotCancellable(InvoiceNotCancellableException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE", ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(ConstraintViolationException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleInvalid(ConstraintViolationException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVALID_REQUEST", ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

**How a refusal reaches the client**
- An invoice that is missing or belongs to another customer gives `404 INVOICE_NOT_FOUND`. The two cases are deliberately identical so a caller can't probe which invoice ids exist.
- An invoice that is not `UNPAID` gives `409 INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE`.
- A non-positive id gives `400 INVALID_REQUEST`.
- All three come back wrapped in `ApiResponse.error(...)`, and the controller has no try/catch.

**Assumptions to check**
- `ApiResponse.error(String code, String message)` exists, matching the `ApiResponse.ok(...)` convention. Adjust the call if the real signature differs.
- `InvoiceStatus` is an enum with an `UNPAID` value, and `Invoice.status()` returns it.
- `markCancelled()` is persisted by JPA dirty checking inside the `@Transactional` method, because the repository only exposes `findById`. If that isn't true, add a `save` call.
- A payment arriving at the same moment as a cancel could race with the status check. If that matters, add `@Version` optimistic locking on `Invoice` or a pessimistic lock in the lookup.

**Convention notes**
- I used records, a `*Dtos` holder, hand-written constructors (no Lombok) and a thin controller returning `ApiResponse.ok`. The ownership and status rules live in the service, and the `@RestControllerAdvice` maps the domain exceptions with no try/catch in the controller.
- There is no pagination or request body here, so the paging clamp and `@Valid` didn't apply. I validated the path id with `@Positive` instead.
